        "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{1!\\,2!}{3!\\,4!\\,5!}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, notice that $5!=5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2!.$ Then, we can cancel the $2!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1!\\,2!}{3!\\,4!\\,5!} & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2!}{(3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1)(4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1)(5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2!)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\,640}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
